WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.170
JEFFREY SUTHERLAND:
In this session,

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.150
we're discussing something
we all know is true.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.250
Security is getting harder.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.980
Threats scale, attackers adapt.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.500
Even when you do the right
things, underlying assumptions

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.440
in your apps or in the
platform create risk.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.810
And AI is transforming
how attackers operate

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.300
and how we all must respond.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.730
In Windows, we're
changing the model.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.150
But this isn't just Microsoft's
problem, nor is it yours.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.010
We're in this together.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.350
We're raising the security
baseline in Windows

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.080
so you can rely on
stronger defaults

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.710
and your customers stay
safer out of the box.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.540
It's a shared responsibility

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.080
that creates a safer
environment for all.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.960
We remove insecure patterns
from the platform.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.990
You build on modern,
supported foundations.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.870
That's how we reduce risk
across the entire ecosystem.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.560
Let's frame the journey.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.410
You'll see our shifts
across three dimensions.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.540
Reducing insecure legacy.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.490
Enforcing code trust by default.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.950
And preparing for the next
generation of cryptography.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.680
Let's start by removing
some legacy code.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.200
NTLM creates real risk.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.370
We all know it.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.410
Many environments
still depend on it.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.680
We're removing that risk while
preserving real-world scenarios

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.950
so you can move forward without
breaking your customers.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.750
Start planning that
transition now

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.330
so your customers don't
feel the impact later.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.470
Now, Mariam will walk you

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.290
through how the platform is
changing and what we're doing

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:32.250
to minimize the impact
on you and your users.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.840
MARIAM GEWIDA: Thanks, Jeffrey.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.780
Like you mentioned, let's
first look at what NTLM is

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.360
and why it's considered
weak and outdated.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.100
NTLM is an
authentication protocol

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.710
that goes all the way
back to the early 2000s.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.240
It has since been deprecated,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.610
meaning it is no
longer undergoing any

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.020
active development.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.110
And organizations are strongly
encouraged to move away from it

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:56.160
and onto more secure
protocols like Kerberos.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.850
Let's go through a typical
NTLM authentication flow.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.510
In this scenario, a client is
trying to access a resource.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.010
It connects to the server.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.730
The server asks who you are,
which is the challenge.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.040
And then the client provides
a string with a username

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.620
and password plus the
response to the challenge.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.370
The server gives access and the
authentication goes through.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.480
But what's the issue?

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.180
Number one, there's no
server client verification.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.460
And so when the client is
connecting to the server,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.470
it doesn't actually know
that it is connecting

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:26.970
to the intended server.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.710
So an attacker can intercept the
request and act as the server.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.230
Problem number two, it
is super weak and old.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.450
It uses super weak
and old crypto.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.160
The message that the
client sends back

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.390
to the server uses an
MD4-derived NT hash and HMAC MD5

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.840
to compute the challenge
response during authentication.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.240
And finally, problem number
three, because of this lack

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.390
of mutual authentication and
the use of outdated crypto,

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.730
an attacker can relay
the challenge response

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.670
to another server without
cracking the password.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.400
So this, combined with
the weak legacy hashing

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.480
and the reusable NT hashes, this
makes NTLM highly susceptible

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.270
to relay and pass
the hash attacks.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:09.600
Knowing this, you might wonder
why NTLM is still prominent

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.220
in so many environments.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.480
Some of the key scenarios

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.880
where NTLM often
becomes the only option

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.790
for authentication are when
there is no line of sight

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.580
to a domain controller, in
local account scenarios,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.770
or in scenarios where
there is an unknown SPN

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.160
or an IP address being used.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.190
So as part of our Windows
security hardening mission,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:30.600
my team is fully committed

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.030
to help organizations
protect their environments

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.830
against common attacks.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.290
And so to do so, we have
prioritized the elimination

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.820
of NTLM in Windows.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.040
However, given the
several key scenarios

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.080
where NTLM becomes
the only option,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.900
we have dedicated our time to
expanding Kerberos support

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.940
in these exact scenarios.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.760
So first, we built IAKerb,
which is an extension

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.570
of Kerberos acting as a wrapper
around Kerberos and enabling it

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.250
in scenarios where the reach

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.440
to a domain controller
is restricted.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.530
We also built LocalKDC,
which is designed to act

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.550
as an authentication
authority where it can talk

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.310
to a SAM database on a given
Windows instance and give

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.860
out tickets for the
identities that are local.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.890
So with IAKerb and LocalKDC,
you can eliminate some

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.220
of the most common uses of NTLM,
further hardening the security

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.030
of your organization
and your environment.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.370
IAKerb and LocalKDC will
be available in client

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.640
and server WIP over the next
few weeks, and you are going

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.860
to be able to
implement and test it.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:33.420
So now let's look at how IAKerb
really works behind the scenes.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.300
In the first step, you see
Kerberos authentication fails

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.540
because there is no line of
sight to the domain controller.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.750
The client then says, okay,
I can still try IAKerb.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.540
So the client communicates
with the server both options

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.150
for authentication
entailing Kerberos.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.640
The server acknowledges
that it can use IAKerb

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.530
and starts the
negotiation process,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.650
first attempting IAKerb,
and it succeeds.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.110
The client then gets the
Kerberos tickets and succeeds

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.600
with Kerberos authentication.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.970
One example where
IAKerb applies is

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.610
when there's a remote
user working from home

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.460
for a corporate that has
public-facing corporate server,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.480
for example.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.240
And so to access the service,
the user has to authenticate

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.900
to the service, and for some
reason, they have VPN issues.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.060
In this case, they cannot
reach the domain controller,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.070
and the domain controller
is behind a firewall,

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.410
so they cannot even
get a Kerberos ticket.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.470
So in this scenario, IAKerb
acts as a proxy for Kerberos,

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.230
and the server was essentially
the middleman between the client

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.320
and the domain controller,
as it forwarded the messages

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.720
between them and enabled
the client to get a ticket

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.310
and present it to the service.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.330
And with that, authentication
with IAKerb is successful.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:42.570
Now let's move on to local
KDC, which is mostly used

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.880
in workgroup or
cluster scenarios.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.150
This works by having every
Windows instance running a local

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.910
KDC that interacts with
the local SAM database

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.790
on the given Windows
system, and it has access

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.260
to the local accounts.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.260
So when the client tries to
authenticate the server,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.080
the server will talk to
the local KDC internally,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.810
and it will be able to mint
tickets for this identity,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.360
enabling the authentication
flow to go on.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.060
The security benefits here
is that it replaces NTLM

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.730
for local accounts, which is
one of the largest scenarios

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.620
where we see NTLM usage today.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.350
Now let's look at how
authentication without NTLM

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.860
and with IAKerb and local
KDC goes in this demo.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.320
Here, I'm going to try
to access a file share

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:26.820
in this standalone setup
with two unjoined machines.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.200
First, let's block NTLM.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.420
Now if we look into
the Wireshark capture,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.930
we will be able to see what the
client advertised to the server.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:38.970
In that advertisement,
we're going to see

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.660
that it advertised IAKerb.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:47.250
Now let's see if we
actually end up using NTLM.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:52.680
They exchange a couple of IAKerb
messages, and eventually we get

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.680
to a message that says,
oh, everything was good.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.290
I was able to authenticate
using IAKerb,

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.290
and authentication succeeds.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:04.110
Now let's move on into what
we have already released,

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:05.610
and it will take
you a step further

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:09.660
into enabling a more secure
authentication protocol

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.100
in your environment.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:14.460
Late last year, we released
the enhanced NTLM auditing

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.410
protocols, which went out to
Server 2025 and Client 24H2.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.420
With the enhanced
auditing, you will be able

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:26.310
to know why NTLM is being
used, where it is being used,

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.730
and who the authentication
request is going from and to.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:33.270
We highly suggest that you
start using NTLM auditing today

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.180
in your environment to know
how NTLM is being used,

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:38.370
and that will take you a
step further into preparing

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:43.200
into the overall disablement
of NTLM in Windows.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.020
We are also excited to announce
that over the next few weeks,

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.260
we will also be releasing
NTLM blocking policies.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.030
These blocking
policies are available

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.710
through the
administrative templates

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.900
under Systems and then NTLM.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.000
With these blocking
policies, you will be able

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.300
to selectively block
specific scenarios of NTLM

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.290
within your environment.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:05.190
So this includes SSO blocking,
includes disabling NTLM

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.320
for domain controllers,
and so forth.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.690
This will take you one
step further into starting

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:13.260
to disable NTLM and looking
at to see what breaks.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.740
With that, with the enablement
of IAKerb and local KDC,

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:20.040
you'll be able to move,
successfully move away

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:23.520
from NTLM in your environment.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.590
And so what can you
start doing now with all

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.850
of these new features?

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:29.460
One, start using the enhanced
NTLM auditing feature,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.990
which we released last year.

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.330
Again, this will tell you
how NTLM is being used

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.940
in your environment and what
you can start doing today

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:38.400
to mitigate those
NTLM scenarios.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.200
Two, you start remediating.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.230
Fix the unknown SPN scenarios.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.090
Start registering your IPs.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.760
Start to move away from
third-party applications

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.130
that are hard-coded to use NTLM.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.040
And then you can start in
step three, piloting IAKerb

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.370
and local KDC in Windows
Insiders for server and client.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.740
Those will be available
in server 2025

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.020
and Client 24H2 and above.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:02.790
So you can start
enabling those features.

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.010
And with the blocking
policies, you will be able

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.710
to see successful Kerberos
authentication even

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:12.180
when NTLM is blocked and in
previously dependent cases.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.100
And then finally,
you can even start

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.920
to fully block NTLM
in your environment.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.760
And you can always reach out
to us at NTLM@microsoft.com

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.040
to report any unique scenarios

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.290
that you identify
NTLM usages in.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.690
And we'll be happy to assist
you in either migrating away

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:31.110
from NTLM in those scenarios or
putting them into our backlog

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.930
of scenarios that we need
to address moving forward.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:35.670
And that's it.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.160
JEFFREY SUTHERLAND: NTLM shows
how we move forward together.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.240
We remove insecure paths and we
provide a working replacement.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.900
Next, we apply that same
idea to code execution.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.670
Even with strong authentication,

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.950
untrusted code can
still reach users.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:53.340
So we're making trusted code the
default experience on Windows.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.370
Jordan will show what that
means and where we need you

00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:00.330
to adopt code signing for all of
your code, including installers,

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:03.990
uninstallers,
scripts, and add-ons.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:04.710
JORDAN GEURTEN: Thanks, Jeffrey.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.920
I'm Jordan at TPM here on the
Windows Platform Integrity team.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.050
Today, I'll be talking
about new driver security

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.530
and app control code integrity
changes coming to Windows.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:14.400
These changes are all

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:18.060
about eliminating common attack
paths, built-in protection.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.970
This means fewer
opportunities for attackers.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.740
Microsoft is tightening
driver trust

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.150
across the Windows platform.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.760
Cross-sign drivers are no
longer trusted by default.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:27.750
We're moving to a model

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:31.320
where WHCP certified drivers
are becoming the baseline.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.630
You may ask why
Microsoft is doing this.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:35.880
Well, the cross-sign driver
program has posed a security

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.520
risk for our partners
and customers for years.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.650
This program had
relaxed partner vetting

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.900
and also didn't have
anti-malware scanning,

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.480
which meant malicious content
was signed and abused.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.300
And the responsibility to
protect signing keys fell

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:52.950
to our partners, which meant
keys were stolen in some cases.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.230
Additionally, the cross-signing
program has been deprecated

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:56.670
since 2011.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:11:00.000
Back in 2016, Windows already
enforced WHCP signing

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.560
for some driver scenarios,

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.920
so we're simply expanding
the enforcement.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:07.350
We're protecting compatibility
for users and systems

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.810
with a curated list of
cross-signed drivers,

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.160
which was informed by trillions

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:14.760
of driver load
diagnostic data signals.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.710
The feature rolls
out in two phases.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:19.560
We start in audit mode, so
Windows can measure impact

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.540
to our users and the systems

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.720
across three reboots
and 100 system hours.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.840
We then move into enforcement,
provided there is no impact

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.000
to our users and the systems.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.300
The driver policy update
is hitting Windows 1124H2

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.050
and newer platforms in the
May 2026 security update.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:40.170
Windows Server 2025
is coming soon.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.750
If you ship drivers,
the action is simple.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.270
Validate under audit mode

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.630
and WHCP certify your
cross-signed drivers now.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.570
Auditing your drivers is
as easy as three steps.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:55.440
The first step is on a system
with a May 2026 update or newer.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.200
First, check the
state of the policy.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:02.550
Here, I've provided PowerShell
commands to easily get that.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:06.270
Step two is to verify your
drivers are WHCP certified using

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.730
tooling like SignTool
or Sigcheck.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.560
If drivers are WHCP signed,

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.860
they will be default
trusted by the policy.

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:17.100
Step three, for non-WHCP
certified drivers,

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.650
you need to test them
against the policy.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:22.200
PowerShell commands have
been provided here.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.480
If you observe no
event log entries,

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.820
your drivers are
trusted by policy.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:28.260
If you see event log entries,

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:30.960
your drivers will be
blocked by the new policy.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.050
The last step is for any
non-WHCP signed drivers,

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:36.660
regardless if they pass
policy, we recommend going

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:39.570
through the WHCP
certification process.

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:40.830
You can start by registering

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.830
at the Hardware Dev
Center, HDC for short.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:47.280
You need to then run
and pass the HLK test

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:49.350
and submit the driver
package to HDC,

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:53.100
or Microsoft will WHCP
sign your drivers.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.470
You want to finish by
distributing the signed driver

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.120
through Windows Update.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.400
Let's follow steps one
through three to learn how

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.010
to verify your drivers against
this new driver policy.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:05.100
The first step is to determine
the state of the policy.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.140
Here, I've written a
script using CI Tool,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.680
which is an inbox utility,
to get the policy state.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:13.650
In my system's case, the
policy is in enforcement mode,

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:15.780
so drivers will be blocked.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:17.670
The next step is to
verify the signatures

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:19.710
on each of my drivers.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:21.960
Here, I have some Hello World
drivers that are written

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:24.870
with various signature
types to validate.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.780
To validate each signature,
I'm going to use SciTool,

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:30.810
which is a tool
available in the SDK.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.750
With these parameters, SciTool
will dump all the signatures

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:37.650
on the driver, including
any catalog signatures.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.200
We can see that my first
driver is cross-signed.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.240
A cross-signed driver
is any signature

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:44.010
with a certificate
chaining to a root

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:46.470
that is not the Microsoft root.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.900
This driver may be
blocked by policy,

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:51.300
as cross-signed drivers are
no longer trusted by default,

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.060
except for an explicit list
for compatibility scenarios.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.550
The second driver
is WHCP certified.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.970
The WHCP certified will
have a certificate

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:04.230
that chains the Microsoft root
CA 2010 in its signature.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.420
We can see that the LEAP
certificate is the Microsoft

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.700
Windows hardware
compatibility publisher.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:11.070
This driver is default
trusted by policy.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:12.840
I don't have to worry about it.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.030
The third driver is dual-signed.

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:19.170
It is cross-signed
and WHCP certified.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:21.450
It is also default
trusted by policy

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:24.450
because it is WHCP signed,
so I don't have to worry

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:26.440
about this one as well.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:31.920
The last step is to test
drivers against the policy.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.470
To verify my drivers, I'm
going to load each one

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:37.260
and evaluate any audit
and block events.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.250
The only one that I'm worried

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.890
about is the exclusively
cross-signed driver,

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:43.620
as it is no longer
trusted by default.

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.110
Here, I have a script to
automate loading my drivers

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.300
and another to pull all
the audit and block events

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.670
for the Windows driver policy.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.760
You can see that the non-WHCP
certified driver is blocked

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:55.170
by the new policy.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:15:00.360
There is a 3076 event for the
audit version of the policy

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.030
and also a 3077 block event

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.670
for the enforcement
mode of the policy.

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.220
I will need to WHCP
certify this driver,

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.470
otherwise my customers will
experience friction while

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:12.420
using it.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:15.030
Switching gears to
application security now.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.640
Application control for
business, or simply app control,

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.070
is Microsoft's application
control security feature

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:21.600
for organizations.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:24.000
App control is built directly
into the Windows kernel

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.120
on desktop and server, which
prevents the need to install

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.810
or download any clunky
agents on top of systems.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:33.570
It can validate kernel
processes like drivers,

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.540
as well as applications and
scripts, to lock down systems

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:40.170
to run only trusted,
authorized code.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.520
We integrate with
Defender products

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.460
to make reporting easier
and to make reputation

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.340
and intelligence-based
decisions.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:50.580
Organizations all over the
globe running Windows 10, 11,

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.280
and Server 2016 or newer
leverage app control

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:55.290
to secure their environments.

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:57.900
Organizations with app
control face friction

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.780
when your applications
are unsigned, however.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:03.450
Help our collective customers
by signing your code

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.840
to make Windows more secure and
reduce friction for our users.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:09.000
Smart app control builds
on top of app control

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.460
and shifts the Windows trust
model from the Wild West

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:14.160
and reactive AV blocks
to a reputation

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:15.810
and trust-based model.

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:18.600
The feature is default-enabled
on Windows 11 on tens

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.880
of millions of consumer
devices, and the number

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:23.970
of protected users is
growing every day.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:25.950
Smart app control
trusts vetted content

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:28.590
from the Microsoft Store,
Windows components,

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:31.170
and drivers from the WHCP
certification program

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:32.970
that we just talked about.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:35.760
It also trusts signed
applications from CAs

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:39.060
in the Microsoft Trusted Root
program and content deemed safe

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:42.150
by Microsoft AI
Reputation Services.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.430
The feature does not
trust unsigned apps

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:48.000
without reputation, as Windows
cannot tell who built it

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.970
or verify the integrity
on the content.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.130
It is critical to
sign your code.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.220
If your code is unsigned and
does not have reputation,

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:58.860
this will result in
friction for Windows users.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:01.950
Let's walk through how AI-driven
app control stops malware.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.110
When a user downloads an
app with good reputation

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:06.780
from the Internet, the app gets
successfully installed and run

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:09.630
on smart app control as
it is considered safe.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.900
When the user downloads an
unsigned app without reputation,

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:16.020
the user ignores any warnings
and tries to install anyways.

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:17.790
Smart app control
protects that user,

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:20.480
and the app is
blocked from running.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.000
In the malicious case, the
user unknowingly downloads a

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:24.560
malicious application.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:26.660
Smart app control determines
that the code is malicious,

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:29.730
and the app is blocked,
thereby protecting the user.

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.230
As I've mentioned several times,

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:34.140
unsigned code causes
frictions for your customers.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:38.040
Your code must be signed to
run smoothly now on Windows.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.610
Artifact signing removes
every excuse for not signing.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.980
The Microsoft-provided signing
service is affordable,

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.920
just $9.99 a month
for the basic tier.

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:49.110
The service is also fully
managed by Microsoft.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:53.250
There are no added costs to you
to manage certificates or keys,

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:55.110
and you don't have to
worry about key rotation

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.730
or protecting key material.

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.800
We do that for you.

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.630
We've also met our
developers where they are.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:04.830
We integrate in CI/CD
pipelines like GitHub and ADO,

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:07.650
and we also have CLI options.

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.630
The service also
uses digest signing,

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.610
so your code does not
leave your system.

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:14.250
Only a hash of the content
is sent to the service,

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.890
so it is fast and secure.

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:19.380
It is also part of the
trusted root program,

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:22.770
which builds reputation
with smart app control.

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:24.990
Finally, we recommend
testing your code

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.850
with smart app control enabled,

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:28.320
which you can now
manually enable

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:32.210
in Windows 11, 24H2, and 25H2.

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:34.430
App control also now
supports a new framework

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:36.920
to make your code
app control aware.

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:38.030
You just need three things

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:41.460
to tailor your features
based on the policy state.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:44.220
The first is an app
control manifest.

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.710
This can be on the
system or remote.

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:50.460
This file will describe the
settings in your application.

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:53.430
Second is the app
setting definition.

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:56.310
This is the name of the
setting and its type.

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:59.880
App control supports
Boolean or string types.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.240
The last step is calling the new
Windows Lockdown Policy API,

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.280
or WLDP for short.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.550
There is one API for
each setting type.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.370
With these three pieces in
place, your organization

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:13.620
or users can write an
app control policy,

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:16.200
which can control security
behavior in your app.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.540
Let's take a look
at how to do this.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:20.940
Here, I've created a
secure plugin host

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.770
with two secure settings.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:24.330
When app control
policies are active

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.220
and the secure
settings are enabled,

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:27.900
the plugins must be signed,

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.600
and the plugins cannot
access the network.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:32.610
Let's see how to build
something similar.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:34.830
We start with an
application manifest.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:37.260
We start by defining
the application ID,

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:39.900
which is the name of
the app, in a setting.

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:42.990
Each definition must have a
setting name, a setting type,

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.450
which can be a bool or a string,

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:48.660
and whether to ignore
audit policies.

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:52.470
The app manifests can be local,
on the system, or remote,

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:54.570
for instance, on GitHub.

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:58.680
Next, we call the WLDP
Get App Settings APIs.

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:02.580
We first import the WLDP
DLL, and we use the API

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:05.940
which maps the setting
type, either the bool,

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:08.400
string list, or string set API.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:12.120
For my app, I'm using
the boolean API.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:15.540
Next, we use the
app manifest ID.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:18.480
This must match the
ID in the manifest.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:22.560
We provide the name of the
setting and the out result.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:24.660
This is the reconciliation
app control does

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:26.850
across all the policies.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:30.690
Finally, the API will return
a status code, an age result.

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.150
This can help you
debug any issues.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.430
The status will be okay
for successful calls.

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.080
Let's take a look at how
this works in practice.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.440
Here we have the secure plugin
host running on a system.

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:45.330
Right now, no
policies are active,

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:48.030
so the plugin host does
not run in secure mode.

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:51.090
The app control author
specifies the path and location

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:54.810
to the app manifest file
for app control to consume.

00:20:54.810 --> 00:20:55.890
They also set the states

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.530
of the settings they wish
to enable or disable.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.950
The IT admin then
deploys the policy,

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:07.530
and on the next WLDP query,

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:10.020
app control verifies
the setting states

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:12.090
and the reconciled
state is returned back

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:13.230
to the application.

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:14.850
This is super exciting
and impactful.

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:16.260
You can see that
your app now runs

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:18.030
in maximum security mode based

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:22.060
on how your customer configured
their app control policy.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:25.370
Now, back to you, Jeffrey.

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:27.200
JEFFREY SUTHERLAND: What
you just saw marks a shift

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:28.610
in expectations.

00:21:28.610 --> 00:21:31.100
Windows is making a
stronger promise to users.

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:33.380
Trusted code runs
with less friction.

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:36.920
That benefits everyone, and it
creates a clear responsibility.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.330
If your code isn't
trusted by default,

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.670
your users will
feel that friction.

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:43.380
Now, underneath all of this,

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:45.770
cryptography forms
the foundation.

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:48.530
That foundation is changing
just as rapidly due

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.140
to the advent of
quantum computing.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.870
Now, Jason will now take us
through what we need to know

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:58.320
about post-quantum cryptography
and how you can stay ahead.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:21:59.250
JASON FISHER: Thanks, Jeffrey.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:02.520
My name is Jason Fisher, the
Group Engineering Manager

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:05.610
for the Cryptography and
Integrity team in Windows.

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:08.280
As mentioned, authentication
isn't the only thing

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.600
that needs to evolve.

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:13.170
Cryptography itself is
facing a long-term shift.

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:15.930
Thus, I am excited to
be here today to talk

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:20.100
about post-quantum
cryptography in Windows.

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:23.100
First off, what is
a quantum computer?

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:24.180
It's a type of computer

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:26.310
that uses quantum
mechanical phenomena,

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:29.940
such as superposition, to
perform operations on data.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:33.210
This allows quantum
computers to use qubits,

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:36.900
which can perform multiple
operations simultaneously,

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:40.860
making quantum computers faster
than classical computers.

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:43.890
They have the potential to
revolutionize many fields,

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:46.740
such as nature and science,
by solving problems

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.900
that are currently
beyond the capabilities

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.640
of classical computers.

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:54.360
However, a sufficiently powerful
quantum computer could undermine

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.160
current encryption methods,

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:59.010
posing challenges
to data security.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.170
Quantum computers can
be used to break many

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:04.080
of the asymmetric encryption
methods that we use today,

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:07.020
such as RSA and elliptic
curve Diffie-Hellman,

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:08.760
considered to be hard problems

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:12.390
that classical computers cannot
solve in a feasible time.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:15.120
Today, our encryption and
communication models rely

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:19.080
on two math problems, discrete
logarithm and factoring,

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.870
which are extremely difficult
to solve and considered

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:24.450
as hard problems for
classical computers.

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:27.630
However, it was proven that
there is a way to solve them

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:31.950
by running Shor's algorithm on
a capable quantum computer.

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:34.740
Post-quantum, or
quantum-safe algorithms,

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:37.920
are thought to be secure
against a crypt-analytic attack

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:39.990
from a quantum computer,
as they are based

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:41.700
on different
mathematical problems,

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:45.400
such as structured lattices
and hash functions.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.560
Quantum computers are in their
early stages, and too small

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:52.500
to crack public-key
cryptography today.

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:53.820
A quantum computer capable

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:56.970
of breaking encryption will
likely require a large number

00:23:56.970 --> 00:23:59.190
of qubits that are
substantially greater

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:01.530
than today's quantum machines.

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:02.820
While the use of
quantum computers

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:05.070
to break cryptography
is still in the future,

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:07.890
the threat is realized today.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:09.300
The effort to inventory

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:13.320
and update all asymmetric
cryptography will arguably be

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:16.770
the most complex multi-year
transition we have

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.390
ever undertaken.

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:20.100
There are increasing
concerns related

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:24.600
to attackers harvesting data
now with a view to being able

00:24:24.600 --> 00:24:27.630
to decrypt it later when
quantum computers are

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:29.370
sufficiently mature.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:33.330
This is referred to as the
harvest-now-decrypt-later style

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:34.620
of attack.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.140
These mainly impact
areas that need storage

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:40.800
of long-term sensitive data,
such as health records,

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:43.890
or hardware and IoT devices,
which will be deployed

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:47.310
in the field for many years,
and likely beyond the point

00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:50.770
where quantum computers can
break their encryption.

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:55.140
On Windows, our efforts have
been concentrated on areas

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:59.010
that offer the greatest leverage
across this broad scope.

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:00.360
To ease in this transition,

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.940
we have been incorporating
post-quantum cryptography

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:04.980
algorithms into Syncrypt,

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:07.620
the core open-source
cryptography library

00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:11.130
that powers our Windows and
Linux operating systems.

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:14.580
In addition, we have been
updating our standard crypto API

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:19.230
service, CNG, or cryptography
next generation.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:22.380
This includes the
NIST-standardized ML-KEM

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:25.140
and ML-DSA algorithms
for encryption

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.600
and digital signatures,
respectively.

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:30.450
We have done so utilizing
modern techniques,

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:34.080
including implementations in
Rust with formal verification,

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:38.220
to ensure the highest grade
of quality and correctness.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.260
While these algorithms
have been standardized,

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:42.600
they are still new,
and there is some risk

00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:44.730
that they could be
broken, either with

00:25:44.730 --> 00:25:45.840
or without the presence

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:49.020
of a sufficiently capable
quantum computer.

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:51.900
Thus, we are additionally
providing support

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:55.830
for the IETF-based hybrid
composite algorithms,

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:58.440
which easily combine
post-quantum algorithms

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:00.150
with their classical variants,

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:03.990
such as ML-KEM
combined with ECDHE.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:07.560
This allows safe integration of
PQC algorithms into applications

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:11.850
and protocols today, without
adding any additional risk.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:14.610
Above the crypto libraries,
we are also pleased to bring

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:19.380
in support for ML-DSA-based
certificate and CMS APIs,

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:22.140
including both certificate
enrollment via Active Directory

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:23.580
Certificate Services

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:26.610
and certificate chain
building through Crypt32.

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:29.970
Lastly, our in-box
implementation of TLS,

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:33.210
S-Channel, has been updated
to support negotiation

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:36.690
of hybrid-based key exchange
groups, a critical piece needed

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:40.380
to migrate TLS 1.3 connections
to be quantum-resistant

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.680
from harvest-now,
decrypt-later attacks.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.620
As global standards and
regulations continue to mature

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:49.890
in this ever-evolving space,
we are committed to continuing

00:26:49.890 --> 00:26:52.140
to update our cryptography
infrastructure

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:55.440
to support customer needs.

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:57.510
Now, let's look at
our first demo,

00:26:57.510 --> 00:27:02.190
showcasing how you can use
our CNG-BCrypt API interface

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.940
to perform ML-DSA signature
and verification.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:07.170
VOICEOVER: In this
demo, we are going

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:11.970
to showcase how you can leverage
our CNG-BCrypt API interface

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:15.720
to generate new ML-DSA
keys and signed messages.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:18.450
We have updated BCrypt
Generate Key Pair

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:22.230
to support the ML-DSA
cryptographic algorithms,

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:25.020
which can be neatly passed
into Bcrypt Sign Hash

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:26.970
and Bcrypt Verify Signature

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:30.450
with the Bcrypt Pad
PQ-DSA Signing Context.

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:33.150
This allows you to easily
update your applications

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:37.470
that are already using
Bcrypt for RSA or ECC today.

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:40.980
Here, you can see, we first call
our configurable application

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:45.960
with ML-DSA 65, producing a
signature of 3,309 bytes.

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:48.930
Now, let's compare
with classic RSA 3K,

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:52.620
producing a signature
of 384 bytes.

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:54.360
This highlights the
size difference

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:57.120
between ML-DSA and RSA.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:59.310
JASON FISHER: For our second
demo, we will now focus

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:02.610
on composite ML-KEM
for encapsulating data

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:05.700
that can be shared with
a peer via Encrypt.

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:06.870
VOICEOVER: In this
demo, we are going

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:10.710
to showcase how you can leverage
our new composite ML-KEM plus

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:14.460
ECC parameter sets and
new Encrypt Encapsulate

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:17.460
and Encrypt Decapsulate
APIs to encrypt

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:21.450
and decrypt content via our
CNG Encrypt API surface.

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:25.380
You start with our standard
Encrypt Create Persisted Key API

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:29.280
to generate new composite
ML-KEM key pairs.

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:32.940
You can then call Encrypt Export
Key to produce a public key

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:34.350
to share with your peer.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:40.350
In this case, we showed
ML-KEM 768 with ECDH-P256,

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:44.130
producing a public
key of 1,249 bytes.

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:46.650
On the receiving end,
the peer can import

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:49.140
that key via Encrypt Import Key.

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:52.710
Once imported, that's when
the peer can leverage our new

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:54.900
Encrypt Encapsulate API

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:58.380
to produce ciphertext
and a shared secret.

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:03.690
In this case, with ML-KEM
768 and ECDH-P256,

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:07.380
the ciphertext was 1,155 bytes.

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:09.060
After sharing the ciphertext,

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:13.710
the originating client can call
our new Encrypt Decapsulate API

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:16.320
to produce the same
shared secret.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.900
JASON FISHER: For our final
demo, we will show an example

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:22.230
of a TLS connection being
updated to negotiate

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:25.920
and communicate over hybrid
key exchange groups.

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:27.690
VOICEOVER: In this final
demo, we are going

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:31.920
to showcase how our Inbox TLS
Library S-Channel has now been

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:35.490
updated to support hybrid
key exchange groups.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:39.090
Today, this requires updating
policy on the device

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:40.560
to configure negotiation

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:43.860
of these new ML-KEM
plus ECDH hybrids.

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:47.520
After configuration, you can
see with this Wireshark trace

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:49.500
that by navigating to a peer,

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:52.620
which also supports
hybrid key exchange,

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:55.950
the appropriate quantum
safe group was selected.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:57.180
JASON FISHER: As
a call to action,

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:00.390
post-quantum cryptography
is not just an upgrade.

00:30:00.390 --> 00:30:01.980
It is a fundamental shift

00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:04.830
to secure the next
era of computing.

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:08.370
Thus, you should start now
as transitioning takes time

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:10.230
and early prep is critical.

00:30:10.230 --> 00:30:12.960
To start now, you should be
planning Windows upgrades

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:16.200
to the latest operating
system, moving to TLS 1.3

00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:19.620
where applicable, updating
your applications for ML-KEM

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:23.220
and ML-DSA, either in
pure or composite mode,

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:25.770
and designing for
crypto agility.

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:28.350
Because algorithms will be
changing in the future,

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.600
the ability to
migrate efficiently

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.000
and safely is critical.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.010
Thank you for your time,
and I am handing off

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:38.010
to Jeffrey to conclude.

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:40.590
JEFFREY SUTHERLAND: Now,
let's bring it all together.

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:43.710
Across Windows, we're making
a set of commitments.

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:47.130
Moving authentication
off of legacy patterns.

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:49.920
Making code trust the default.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:55.050
And advancing cryptography so it
stays ahead of future threats.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.270
Our shared responsibility
is simple.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:30:59.550
Your software stays
secure by default

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:02.310
because the platform
starts secure by default.

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:05.550
That only works if
we both do our part.

00:31:05.550 --> 00:31:08.820
For you, that means move
off of legacy dependencies.

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:10.530
Sign everything you ship.

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.200
Design your apps for a world
that's fundamentally different

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.420
because of quantum
computing and AI.

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:17.850
When you take those steps,
your apps run smoothly

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.710
and your users stay protected.

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:23.880
If you don't, friction shows up
and your users feel it first.

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:26.220
This is how we raise
the bar together.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:27.690
We commit to
delivering a stronger,

00:31:27.690 --> 00:31:29.100
more secure foundation.

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:30.480
We ask you to meet us there

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.970
so we can protect the
customers we share.

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:34.350
Because security only works

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:37.690
when the whole ecosystem
moves forward together.
